#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e06efb1-c614-5df4-a96d-54a8bc3e08a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287444} {:answer/id #uuid "286f6836-e5cf-5ad9-91af-0d37c393b681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287445} {:answer/id #uuid "ef55e054-3b41-52ee-aef5-22ac31bec600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287446} {:answer/id #uuid "8494c0c2-11ad-5038-8e44-e8984fcbc27b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287447} {:answer/id #uuid "34f74688-ce7b-5d2e-ac6b-ba72b6e3921a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "286f6836-e5cf-5ad9-91af-0d37c393b681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287445} :answer-field/id #uuid "3af8268c-56b5-5e72-85f4-2e16fa02fce9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c36b197-027f-56c2-a704-60e8c33c39b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-15647" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x+1}$ and the point $P(21,4)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(21,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt[3]{3x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{2/3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(21) & =\\frac{1}{(64)^{2/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(21,4)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(4,21)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(21)} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b91cc901-d4a8-5750-bee2-35b539bb8889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287451} {:answer/id #uuid "abf62472-d96a-5ca8-866a-dad158af934e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abf62472-d96a-5ca8-866a-dad158af934e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287452} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1a287c1-3c09-5131-a2d0-07ea90edf310" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "284514bc-fbdd-5962-b8a5-481ef5bc2a15" :question/math-academy-id "q-342588" :question/problem "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $-2$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-7$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(1)=2$ and ${f}^{′}(2)=-7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-7)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93d6c80e-e791-55aa-b703-f982b38ee085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93d6c80e-e791-55aa-b703-f982b38ee085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287455} :answer-field/id #uuid "d62851f2-84e2-56c0-8a05-177da63414c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0036795-b710-5f8f-94db-babdb3b4d3c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-342592" :question/problem "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $-8$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(0)=2$ and ${f}^{′}(2)=-8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-8)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5809f2a-705f-56ad-89df-26eff0e73638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287458} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4899d4-2b6e-5d3b-af50-d9fb3cd450ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5809f2a-705f-56ad-89df-26eff0e73638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287458} :answer-field/id #uuid "933a8f66-b4f5-56c5-9264-4e3259c3756c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03828b42-335e-5615-88ca-ef552635348d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342624" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={5}^{x}+3.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ {5}^{x}+3 & =4 \\\\ {5}^{x} & =1 \\\\ {5}^{x} & ={5}^{0} \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={5}^{x}+3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & ={5}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a8647fa-d56d-55e8-86fd-f6210ac630a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a8647fa-d56d-55e8-86fd-f6210ac630a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287462} :answer-field/id #uuid "a93944aa-d827-5aa6-bd4d-ad8afdb1f5ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77a5f47e-8f48-52c6-b3b5-dcc98f89fccd" :question/math-academy-id "q-342625" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ (x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)+\\frac{x+3}{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+\\frac{2+3}{2-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =0+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
